WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.206 --> 00:00:01.305
(dreamy mysterious music)

00:00:01.305 --> 00:00:03.365
- [Host] Easter Island isn't
the only place in the world

00:00:03.365 --> 00:00:06.479
with some mysterious stone faces.

00:00:06.479 --> 00:00:09.143
In the highest peak of the
Eastern Taurus mountain range

00:00:09.143 --> 00:00:11.393
in Turkey lies Nemrut Dag.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.814
Nemrut Dag is a temple
tomb believed to be built

00:00:18.814 --> 00:00:22.370
by the late Hellenistic King Antiochos.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.149
He reigned from 69 to 34 BC

00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:28.773
and built the temple
to commemorate himself.

00:00:28.773 --> 00:00:31.386
The monument features statues of the king,

00:00:31.386 --> 00:00:35.553
lions, eagles, and various
Greek, Armenian, and Medes gods,

00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:40.494
with each measuring almost 30 feet tall.

00:00:41.652 --> 00:00:43.435
The heads of these
sculptures once sat atop

00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:45.230
their stone bodies, but at some point

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.480
in time, they were removed.

00:00:48.779 --> 00:00:50.805
Nemrut Dag went lost to memory

00:00:50.805 --> 00:00:54.755
until it was rediscovered
by a geologist in 1881,

00:00:54.755 --> 00:00:58.922
and in 1987 it was designated
a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:02.226
And while there are those that believe

00:01:02.226 --> 00:01:05.309
these figures hold a deep
mythological significance,

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:08.298
how these stone structures
were physically constructed

00:01:08.298 --> 00:01:11.131
without modern tools is a mystery.

00:01:19.356 --> 00:01:22.606
(echoing digital beep)

